            <li>Showing proof on one platform from the start.</li>
            <li>Building assets, not only chasing salary.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="container section">
      <h2>The 3 gates before you say you are ready</h2>
      <div class="card gate-card">
        <div class="gate-row">
          <strong>Gate 1: Proof of skill</strong>
          <p>Build two role-relevant projects with clear outcomes.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="gate-row">
          <strong>Gate 2: Proof of communication</strong>
          <p>Create one 30-second and one 2-minute pitch in plain English.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="gate-row">
          <strong>Gate 3: Proof of value</strong>
          <p>Get feedback from three experts, buyers, or hiring managers.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="line strong">
        Passing these three gates with a guide gets you to a high-income skill portfolio and real opportunities faster than passing them alone.
      </p>

      <div class="cta-pair">
        <a class="btn-gold pulse" href={guidanceUrl}>Book continuous career guidance</a>
        <a class="btn-outline cta-assessment-lite" href={assessmentsUrl}>Use assessments only as one input</a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="container section">
      <h2>A focused execution plan that beats overthinking</h2>
      <div class="pilot-grid">
        <div class="card pilot-card">
          <span>Phase 1</span>
          <p>Pick two target roles and one backup role.</p>
          <p>Run the scorecard for all three.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card pilot-card">
          <span>Phase 2</span>
          <p>Learn the core tools for one chosen path.</p>
          <p>Build your first mini proof project.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card pilot-card">
          <span>Phase 3</span>
          <p>Build the second project with better quality.</p>
          <p>Ask three people for honest feedback.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card pilot-card">
          <span>Phase 4</span>
          <p>Apply for internships, gigs, or entry roles.</p>
          <p>Keep one daily deep-work block for learning.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="container section">
      <h2>How to talk to parents without turning it into a fight</h2>
      <p class="line">
        Many students are not fighting only confusion.
      </p>
      <p class="line">
        They are also fighting pressure at home.
      </p>

      <div class="card">
        <h3>Talk with evidence, not only emotion</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Show the role you want, not only the degree name.</li>
          <li>Show cost, likely return, and backup plans.</li>
          <li>Show a project plan and timeline of milestones.</li>
          <li>Show the skills you will build outside the classroom too.</li>
          <li>Show that you are not avoiding hard work, only avoiding blind risk.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="card" style="margin-top:1rem;">
        <h3>A script to open the conversation</h3>
        <p class="line">"I want to talk about [target role], not just the degree name. Here is what it actually costs, here is what real people in this role earn early on, here is my backup if it does not work out, and here is the first project I want to build to test it before we spend the full amount."</p>
        <p class="line">Filling in the blanks in that sentence forces you to do the research first — which is usually what turns a fight into a real conversation.</p>
      </div>

      <p class="line">
        If money pressure is the real disagreement underneath the degree argument, <a href="/blog/career-guidance/should-you-choose-a-career-for-money/">should you choose a career for money</a> lays out that specific trade-off honestly. For a parent-facing conversation with a guide instead of alone, <a href="https://futurecareerschool.com/services/career-counselling-and-career-guidance/career-guidance-after-12th/">career guidance after 12th</a> is built for exactly this stage.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="container section">
      <h2>What to do if you already chose the wrong path</h2>
      <p class="line">
        Many students think one wrong choice ruins everything.
      </p>
      <p class="line">
        That is not true.
      </p>
      <p class="line">
        A weak start can still become a strong path if you stack better skills on top of it.
      </p>

      <div class="grid-two">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Do this first</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>List the transferable skills your current course is already giving you.</li>
            <li>Add one strong market skill on top.</li>
            <li>Build one visible project around that stack.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Do not do this</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Do not stay frozen because of sunk cost.</li>
            <li>Do not keep paying for a weak path without upgrading your real value.</li>
            <li>Do not wait for confidence before starting proof work.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="line">
        Run the option you are stuck weighing through <a href="/blog/career-guidance/factors-to-consider-when-choosing-a-career/">which factor is important while choosing a career</a>, or use the structured comparison in <a href="/blog/career-guidance/what-career-should-i-choose/">what career should I choose: the 5-step decision ladder</a> if you now have two or three real alternatives instead of one wrong path and silence.
      </p>
    </section>

